Patric Mueller wrote:
> Ray Kulhanek <kulhanek.5@wright.edu> wrote:
>
>> People have been tinkering with the Nethack code since it was called
>> Hack, and they won't be stopping any time soon.
>
> They probably won't. Although C isn't as popular as it once was, there
> is still almost always a C compiler for any new platform and OS and
> that won't change any time soon.

I figure evenutally someone will recode it in C++ or another more
current language and then there will be a burst of innovation.

On the other hand software has a life cycle curve that effects entire
fields.  The field of roguelike games reached maturity at some point and
the rate of new games and new features dropped.  It's what happens in
any software field - Rapid progress then maturity with little change
then eventual replacement.

Folks wanting new releases are not comfortable with the point that
roguelikes are on that lifecycle curve.  A new release would be very
nice, but am I going to stop playing Nethack just because there is not
new release yet?  Not until something better comes along and I've
already decided that the graphical type games don't compete for me,


